'''wood-wren''' (wu̇d′ren), ''n.'' '''1.''' Either one of two small woodland birds of Europe, belonging to the subfamily ''Sylviinæ.'' {{smaller|(''a'') The willow-warbler or willow-wren, ''Phylloscopus trochilus.'' (''b'') The true wood-warbler, or yellow willow-wren, ''Phylloscopus sibilatrix'':}}
[[FIGURE]]
{{c|{{smaller|Yellow Wood-wren (''Phylloscopus sibilatrix'').}}}}
{{smaller|the preferable use of the name. The two species, though quite distinct, are much alike and often confounded. Neither is a wren in a proper sense.}} '''2†.''' A supposed species of true wren, described by Audubon in 1834 as ''Troglodytes americanus'', but not different from the common house-wren of the United States.

'''wood-wroth''' (wöd′rôth), ''a.'' Angry to the extent of madness. [Scotch.]
{{smaller block|<poem>When he saw her dear heart’s blood,
A’ ''wood-wroth'' waxed he.</poem>
{{right|''Lord Thomas and Fair Annet'' (Allingham’s Ballad-Book).}}}}

