'''woody''' (wu̇d′i), ''a.'' [Early mod. E. also ''woodie, woddy''; < ME. ''wody, wod,'' woody; < ''wood''<sup>1</sup> + ''-y''<sup>1</sup>.] '''1.''' Abounding with wood; wooded: as, ''woody'' land; a ''woody'' region.
{{smaller block|It is all ''woddy'', but by the Sea side Southward there are sands like downes.
{{right|Quoted in ''Capt. John Smith’s'' Works, II. 277.}}
<poem>Oft in glimmering bowers and glades
He met her, and in secret shades
Of ''woody'' Ida’s inmost grove.</poem>
{{right|''Milton'', Il Penseroso, l. 29.}}
A slanting ray lingered on the ''woody'' crests of the precipices that overhung some parts of the river, giving greater depth to the dark-gray and purple of their rocky sides.
{{right|''Irving'', Sketch-Book, p. 438.}}}}
'''2.''' Pertaining or belonging to the woods; dwelling or situated in the woods; peculiar to a wood or forest; sylvan; woodland; woodsy.
{{smaller block|<poem>All the Satyres scorne their ''woody'' kind.</poem>
{{right|''Spenser'', F. Q., I. vi. 18.}}
The Brachmanes, which he in his Indian trauels had found in a ''woodie'' solitarinesse.
{{right|''Purchas'', Pilgrimage, p. 367.}}}}
'''3.''' Consisting of or containing wood; ligneous: as, the ''woody'' parts of plants.
{{smaller block|Herbs are those plants whose stalks are soft, and have nothing ''woody'' in them, as grass, sowthistle, and hemlock.
{{right|''Locke'', Elem. of Nat. Philos., ix.}}}}
'''4.''' Peculiar to or characteristic of wood: as, a ''woody'' scent or flavor.—'''Glandular woody fiber.'''
